WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.512
Run the department query and focus first on row grain.

00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:06.207
Each row represents one department.

00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:09.627
The outer aggregate functions summarize admission rows.

00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:16.297
The correlated scalar subquery uses the current department name to count open admissions only for that

00:00:16.297 --> 00:00:20.387
department. This is a useful pattern for dashboards and grouped summaries.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:25.487
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.487 --> 00:00:31.572
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:33.897
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.897 --> 00:00:39.822
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:39.822 --> 00:00:45.932
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.932 --> 00:00:48.232
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.232 --> 00:00:54.677
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
